# Keep build products out of the build context.
#
# Every Containerfile here does `COPY apexd/ /build/apexd/` and then builds it.
# A developer's local `cargo build` leaves ~440 MB of host-built artefacts in
# apexd/target, and without this they are shipped into the context and land on
# top of the container's own target directory — host rustc and glibc output
# seeding a container build. CI never noticed because a fresh checkout has no
# target/; a local build is where it bites, and "works in CI, wrong locally" is
# the harder direction to debug.
#
# ANCHORED, and this is not a style preference. `**/target/` matches a directory
# named `target` at ANY depth, including `apexd/apex-backup-core/src/target/` —
# a SOURCE module holding the backup backends (fs, ssh, bucket). Excluding it
# from the build context does not fail the COPY; it fails the compile, much
# later, with a message that names neither the context nor this file:
#
#     error[E0583]: file not found for module `target`
#       --> apex-backup-core/src/lib.rs:45:1
#
# That is run 35582968952's `base` job, exit 101, which skipped `image` and
# `qcow2` behind it. `.gitignore` made this EXACT mistake with a bare `target/`
# for the life of the repo and carries its own note about it — the same trap,
# one file over. List the real Cargo build directories explicitly; when a new
# crate gains one, add it here rather than reaching for a wildcard.
apexd/target/
apexd/target-clippy/
installer/target/
windows-installer/target/
/target/
/target-clippy/

.git/
